<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Moselle</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Moselle is a river of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, Luxembourg and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, about 545 kilometres long. Rising in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vosges">Vosges</a>, it flows past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a>, forms part of the border between Luxembourg and Germany and joins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koblenz">Koblenz</a>. Its valley, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a> as its chief city, was important in Roman and medieval times and is known for its vineyards.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moselle.jpg" alt="Moselle" width="320" height="317" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Areks · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schweicher_Annaberg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 959 Archbishop Bruno of Cologne, brother of Otto I and duke of Lotharingia, divided it into Lower Lorraine in the north and Upper Lorraine along the <b>Moselle</b> and upper Meuse, entrusting the south to Frederick, count of Bar.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-lorraine">Duchy of Lorraine · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Schengen Agreement that abolished border checks across much of Europe is named after a small wine-growing village in Luxembourg, where it was signed on a boat on the <b>Moselle</b> in 1985.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-luxembourg">Grand Duchy of Luxembourg · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>After about 450 BCE the princely seats were abandoned and power passed to warrior elites farther north and west, in the Marne, the <b>Moselle</b> and the Rhineland, who were buried with two-wheeled chariots.</blockquote><a href="/culture/celts">Celts · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>There is no agreement on exactly where it began, but there is broad consensus that its centre lay on the north-western edge of the Hallstatt world, north of the Alps, between the valleys of the Marne and <b>Moselle</b> and the nearby Rhineland.</blockquote><a href="/culture/la-tene-culture">La Tène culture · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>His descendants, and from 1136 the counts of Namur and later Limburg lines, expanded the county through the Ardennes and <b>Moselle</b> valley.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-luxembourg">County of Luxembourg</a></li><li><blockquote>The counts ruled villages, vineyards and castles across the Hunsrück and the lower Nahe and <b>Moselle</b>, balancing between the archbishops of Trier and Mainz and the Electoral Palatinate.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-sponheim">County of Sponheim</a></li></ul></section>
